<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harriet Beecher Stowe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Harriet Elisabeth Beecher Stowe, Harriet Beecher</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harriet Beecher Stowe was an American author and abolitionist, born in Litchfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, the daughter of the Calvinist preacher Lyman Beecher. In 1832 she moved to Cincinnati, where she met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> who had suffered in attacks on the city's Black residents, and in 1836 she married Calvin Ellis Stowe. Her novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uncle-toms-cabin">Uncle Tom's Cabin</a> (1852), which depicts the harsh conditions of enslaved African Americans, reached millions as a novel and play, energising anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> forces in the North and provoking anger in the South. She wrote over 30 books and met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> in 1862. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hartford-connecticut">Hartford, Connecticut</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harriet-beecher-stowe.jpg" alt="Harriet Beecher Stowe" width="330" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not specified · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beecher-Stowe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Harriet Beecher Stowe</b>'s Uncle Tom's Cabin (1852) has Tom sing it, with a verse not by Newton that had been passed down orally in African American communities.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li></ul></section>
